// ============================================================================
// PROBE — STAGE 2: CAUSAL RANKING (the mechanism that names ONE root cause)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE RULE, in one sentence a beginner can defend on stage:
//
//   "Among the services that changed, the root cause is the one with NO
//    misbehaving dependency of its own."
//
// ONE signal does the work, and it is structural, not statistical:
//   TOPOLOGY — a service whose own dependencies are all healthy cannot be a
//   victim of anything downstream; failure propagates from callee UP to caller.
//   So a service with zero anomalous dependencies IS the cause.
//
// ⚠ TIMING IS NOT THE DECIDER — this was corrected after adversarial testing
// (research/09-GATE2-REALITY-CHECK.md, Finding 1). For a SYNCHRONOUS fault the
// root and its victims fail on the SAME requests, milliseconds apart, so their
// change points land in the SAME bucket and cannot be ordered at all. The SORT
// below therefore does NOT pick the root out of the victims -- WHERE is_sink
// does that. The SORT only orders MULTIPLE INDEPENDENT ROOTS, which is exactly
// the concurrent-incident case.
//
// It is a structural rule, NOT a weighted score. There are no magic weights to
// defend, and the answer is falsifiable.
//
// Inputs (both `index.mode: lookup`, single-shard — required by LOOKUP JOIN [F5]):
//   probe-anomaly-current  : one doc per anomalous service, written by Stage 1.
//                            Fields: svc, change_ts, cp_pvalue, signal
//                            PLUS a duplicated key field `callee` == svc, so the
//                            same set can be joined from the callee side.
//   probe-service-graph    : one doc per DIRECTED EDGE. Fields: caller, callee.
//
// LOOKUP JOIN after STATS/CHANGE_POINT is only restricted for CROSS-CLUSTER
// queries. PROBE is single-cluster, so this shape is legal. [F5] [UNTESTED]
// ============================================================================

FROM probe-anomaly-current

// --- Topological step: pull in each anomalous service's DEPENDENCIES ---------
// Join key must already exist as a column with the matching name. [F5]
| EVAL caller = svc
| LOOKUP JOIN probe-service-graph ON caller
// -> one row per (anomalous service, one of its callees). Services with no
//    outgoing edges keep a single row with callee = null.

// --- Is that dependency ITSELF anomalous? -----------------------------------
// Second join, against the anomaly set keyed by `callee`. A non-null
// dep_change_ts means "this dependency is also misbehaving".
| LOOKUP JOIN probe-anomaly-current-by-callee ON callee
| RENAME change_ts AS dep_change_ts

// --- Collapse back to one row per anomalous service -------------------------
// COUNT() ignores nulls, so this counts only the ANOMALOUS dependencies.
| STATS anomalous_deps = COUNT(dep_change_ts),
        total_deps     = COUNT(callee),
        change_ts      = MIN(change_ts),
        cp_pvalue      = MIN(cp_pvalue),
        signals        = VALUES(signal)
    BY svc

// --- THE RULE ---------------------------------------------------------------
// A service with zero anomalous dependencies is a SINK in the anomalous
// subgraph: nothing it depends on is misbehaving, so nothing upstream explains
// it. That is a root-cause candidate.
| EVAL is_sink = anomalous_deps == 0

| EVAL verdict = CASE(
      is_sink AND total_deps == 0, "root_cause_candidate_leaf",
      is_sink,                     "root_cause_candidate",
                                   "downstream_victim")

// LOOKUP JOIN output order is NOT guaranteed, so SORT must come AFTER the
// joins — never before. [F5 usage notes]
//
// `WHERE is_sink` is what NAMES the root cause. The SORT below only orders
// independent roots when there is more than one (see the timing note above).
| WHERE is_sink == true
| SORT change_ts ASC, cp_pvalue ASC
| KEEP svc, verdict, change_ts, cp_pvalue, anomalous_deps, total_deps, signals
| LIMIT 5
